
File: wave.info, Node: Top, Up: (dir)


MacWave installs each package into its own versioned directory under the
install root, keeps every version side by side, and maintains
unversioned links so a package can be called with or without a version.
Packages are fetched from a mirror or from MacWave's own bottle store;
nothing is compiled on the user's machine.

* Menu:

* install::	Install a package, or one specific version of it.
* upgrade::	Upgrade a package to its latest version; with --all, every
        installed package. Old versions are kept — -X,
        --remove-old-versions removes them in the same run.
* uninstall::	Uninstall a package version. The unversioned link falls back
        to the highest remaining version, and goes away with the
        last one.
* list::	List the packages you have installed, with their versions.
* search::	Search the online package index.
* info::	Show author, description, homepage and the installed /
        available versions.
* selfupdate::	Update MacWave itself to the version published in the
        self-update repository.
* link::	Create a versionless shortcut, or re-point it at another
        installed version. Installs and uninstalls keep it up to
        date afterwards.
* unlink::	Remove the versionless shortcut. A later uninstall will not
        re-create it.
* linkquery::	Show which version the versionless shortcut points at.
* mirror::	Show the address each kind of data is fetched from,
        including the built-in defaults for anything not overridden
        by an environment variable.
* latest::	Compare what you have with the newest version in the index;
        nothing is downloaded.
* shellenv::	Print the shell code that prepends MacWave's directories to
        PATH. You eval it once from your shell profile; it does
        nothing on its own.
* version::	Print the installed MacWave version.
* manual::	Show the full manual — the same text that ships as man pages
        and info files — or just one command's section.

* GLOBAL FLAGS::

  the complete manual。--zh to read the Chinese edition


File: wave.info, Node: GLOBAL FLAGS, Up: Top


  -s, --silent
      Print nothing at all; the exit code is the signal (beats -v;
      install / upgrade / uninstall / link / unlink / selfupdate)

  -S, --show-error
      With -s, still print the error when a command fails (without the
      🌊, so scripts can parse it)

  -C, --continue
      Resume an interrupted download

  --proxy <string>
      HTTP/HTTPS proxy (e.g. http://127.0.0.1:8080)

  --skip-ssl
      Skip SSL certificate verification (insecure)

  --limit-rate <string>
      Limit download speed (e.g. 200K, 1M, 5M; a bare number means
      bytes)

  --max-time <string>
      Maximum total time for one request (40s, 1min, 1min20s, 2h; a bare
      number means seconds)

  --connect-timeout <string>
      Maximum time for the handshake

  --content-time <string>
      Maximum time to receive data

  --ver <string>
      Install a specific version (install only; --ver=1.0 works too)

  -F, --force
      Install anyway when the running macOS is older than the package
      declares

  --install-python-from-source
      Fetch the bundled Python from its upstream URL instead of
      MacWave's own repository

  MACWAVE_PIP_INDEX
      Env var. pip index for the Python deps; default pypi.org/simple



File: wave.info, Node: install, Next: upgrade, Up: Top


Install a package, or one specific version of it.

  -C, --continue
      Resume an interrupted download

  --proxy <string>
      HTTP/HTTPS proxy (e.g. http://127.0.0.1:8080)

  --skip-ssl
      Skip SSL certificate verification (insecure)

  --limit-rate <string>
      Limit download speed (e.g. 200K, 1M, 5M; a bare number means
      bytes)

  --max-time <string>
      Maximum total time for one request (40s, 1min, 1min20s, 2h; a bare
      number means seconds)

  --connect-timeout <string>
      Maximum time for the handshake

  --content-time <string>
      Maximum time to receive data

  --unlink
      Cancel the unversioned link for this package

  -F, --force
      Install anyway when the running macOS is older than the package
      declares

  -v, --verbose
      Verbose mode

Examples:

     wave install jq
     wave install ffmpeg@6.1
     wave install jq --limit-rate 1M
     wave install jq --max-time=1min


File: wave.info, Node: upgrade, Next: uninstall, Prev: install, Up: Top


Upgrade a package to its latest version; with --all, every installed
package. Old versions are kept — -X, --remove-old-versions removes them
in the same run.

  -a, --all
      Upgrade every installed package that has a newer version

  -X, --remove-old-versions
      Remove the older versions as part of the upgrade

  -C, --continue
      Resume an interrupted download

  --proxy <string>
      HTTP/HTTPS proxy (e.g. http://127.0.0.1:8080)

  --skip-ssl
      Skip SSL certificate verification (insecure)

  --limit-rate <string>
      Limit download speed (e.g. 200K, 1M, 5M; a bare number means
      bytes)

  --max-time <string>
      Maximum total time for one request (40s, 1min, 1min20s, 2h; a bare
      number means seconds)

  --connect-timeout <string>
      Maximum time for the handshake

  --content-time <string>
      Maximum time to receive data

  -F, --force
      Install anyway when the running macOS is older than the package
      declares

  -v, --verbose
      Verbose mode

Examples:

     wave upgrade jq
     wave upgrade jq -X
     wave upgrade --all
     wave upgrade jq --remove-old-versions --max-time=2min


